WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.583
If the nerve's response is built from miniatures, why can't you just see them?

2
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:13.407
In normal calcium, one impulse releases dozens of units or more at once.

3
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:16.313
They merge into one smooth response.

4
00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:19.693
It is like a downpour, where you cannot count the drops.

5
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:22.713
To count drops, you need a drizzle.

6
00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:29.047
So del Castillo and Katz lowered calcium and raised magnesium in the bath around a

7
00:00:29.047 --> 00:00:30.047
frog junction.

8
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.350
That cut release to a trickle.

9
00:00:33.317 --> 00:00:36.137
Now an impulse often produced nothing at all.

10
00:00:36.757 --> 00:00:37.757
That is called a failure.

11
00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:40.857
When a response did come, it was small.

12
00:00:41.577 --> 00:00:44.457
So they stimulated many times and sorted the sizes.

13
00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:47.147
Picture a histogram.

14
00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:49.967
Response size runs along the bottom.

15
00:00:50.887 --> 00:00:52.687
The number of trials runs up the side.

16
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:58.167
If release were a smooth flow, just turned down, what shape would this histogram

17
00:00:58.167 --> 00:00:59.167
have?

18
00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:09.197
One simple smooth-flow model would give one smooth spread of sizes, with no

19
00:01:09.197 --> 00:01:10.557
regularly spaced peaks.

20
00:01:11.217 --> 00:01:14.437
The telling sign of packets would be peaks spaced at one miniature.

21
00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:16.990
That is not what they found.

22
00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.210
Watch the trials drop in.

23
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:21.733
A tall bar builds at zero.

24
00:01:22.713 --> 00:01:23.713
These are the failures.

25
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:27.127
A bump grows near the size of one miniature.

26
00:01:27.967 --> 00:01:30.207
The miniature histogram lines up over it.

27
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.620
A smaller bump grows near twice that size.

28
00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.160
A smaller one again near three times.

29
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:42.670
The bumps are broad, because one unit is not exactly the same size every time, and

30
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:43.710
the recording has noise.

31
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.440
But the sizes cluster near whole-number multiples of the miniature.

32
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.740
The response comes in packets.

33
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.500
Each packet is called a quantum.

34
00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.460
Its effect on the muscle, the miniature, is the quantal size,

35
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.680
A quantum holds thousands of acetylcholine molecules.

36
00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.340
It was later tied to the contents of one vesicle.

37
00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:07.833
It is not one molecule.

38
00:02:08.867 --> 00:02:11.307
Think of coins dropped into a jar on a scale.

39
00:02:12.187 --> 00:02:13.247
You cannot see the coins.

40
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.480
But if every reading is a multiple of one coin's weight, you can count them.

41
00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:25.493
That gives the one idea of this lesson: each response ≈ count × size.

42
00:02:26.517 --> 00:02:30.537
The nerve terminal sets the count, Calcium sets it steeply.

43
00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.430
One vesicle's transmitter, acting on the receptors, sets the size,

44
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.630
Big responses fall short of this rule.

45
00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.830
A later chapter shows why.

46
00:02:41.783 --> 00:02:44.383
The count is random from impulse to impulse.

47
00:02:45.203 --> 00:02:46.503
What we track is its average,

48
00:02:48.233 --> 00:02:50.653
So when transmission changes, ask first.

49
00:02:51.573 --> 00:02:53.493
Did the count change, the size, or both?
